WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.873
Let's check Cedar Vale's Solar Support draft

00:00:02.873 --> 00:00:04.181
before it goes for review.

00:00:04.431 --> 00:00:06.800
We'll match the proposal, check the terms,

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:08.399
and confirm who acts next.

00:00:08.649 --> 00:00:10.441
This is an internal draft check;

00:00:10.691 --> 00:00:12.633
customer acceptance comes later.

00:00:13.633 --> 00:00:15.306
You'll need the source proposal and an

00:00:15.306 --> 00:00:18.281
existing Contract draft, plus access to edit

00:00:18.281 --> 00:00:18.967
Contracts.

00:00:19.217 --> 00:00:22.114
Our fictional source is Solar Support Proposal,

00:00:22.114 --> 00:00:25.289
version one: one thousand US dollars a month

00:00:25.289 --> 00:00:26.406
for twelve payments.

00:00:26.656 --> 00:00:28.085
Keep it beside the draft.

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:31.660
In HubSpot, go to Revenue, then Contracts.

00:00:31.910 --> 00:00:34.811
Open Cedar Vale, Solar Support, and confirm

00:00:34.811 --> 00:00:35.793
its Draft status.

00:00:36.043 --> 00:00:38.494
Battery Monitoring is another agreement for

00:00:38.494 --> 00:00:40.919
the same customer, so check the service

00:00:40.919 --> 00:00:42.571
name as well as the customer.

00:00:42.821 --> 00:00:44.207
Choose Edit contract.

00:00:45.233 --> 00:00:47.581
Match the seller, customer and contact to the

00:00:47.581 --> 00:00:50.756
proposal: BeaconGrid, Cedar Vale and Alex

00:00:50.756 --> 00:00:51.249
Morgan.

00:00:51.499 --> 00:00:53.693
Then check the associated Solar Support

00:00:53.693 --> 00:00:54.038
deal.

00:00:54.288 --> 00:00:56.720
That association helps you find the sale;

00:00:56.970 --> 00:00:58.814
it doesn't guarantee every field is

00:00:58.814 --> 00:00:59.551
synchronized.

00:00:59.801 --> 00:01:02.599
If the details differ, ask Sales to confirm

00:01:02.599 --> 00:01:04.324
the current source before continuing.

00:01:05.333 --> 00:01:07.777
Here, the unit price is one thousand dollars

00:01:07.777 --> 00:01:10.052
per month, with quantity one and twelve

00:01:10.052 --> 00:01:10.624
payments.

00:01:10.874 --> 00:01:13.018
So the proposed term value is twelve

00:01:13.018 --> 00:01:13.925
thousand dollars.

00:01:14.175 --> 00:01:16.436
That total is not an invoice due today.

00:01:16.686 --> 00:01:19.280
Check price, quantity, billing frequency and

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:20.419
payment count together.

00:01:21.433 --> 00:01:23.456
The proposed service runs from November

00:01:23.456 --> 00:01:26.206
first, twenty twenty-six, through October

00:01:26.206 --> 00:01:28.175
thirty-first, twenty twenty-seven.

00:01:28.425 --> 00:01:30.897
Compare the service dates and effective date

00:01:30.897 --> 00:01:31.753
with the proposal.

00:01:32.003 --> 00:01:32.963
They match here;

00:01:33.213 --> 00:01:35.517
another agreement may use different dates.

00:01:36.533 --> 00:01:38.727
Finance handles billing separately in this

00:01:38.727 --> 00:01:41.291
example, so HubSpot Billing stays off.

00:01:41.541 --> 00:01:44.067
The native message says no invoices will be

00:01:44.067 --> 00:01:45.210
created or sent.

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:47.854
Keep that choice deliberate: checking this

00:01:47.854 --> 00:01:50.729
draft doesn't connect Finance's billing system

00:01:50.729 --> 00:01:52.116
or authorize a charge.

00:01:53.133 --> 00:01:55.456
Return to the record and reopen the draft

00:01:55.456 --> 00:01:56.931
to check the saved values.

00:01:57.181 --> 00:01:59.528
Compare the customer, service, price,

00:01:59.528 --> 00:02:01.678
payment count, dates and Billing off with

00:02:01.678 --> 00:02:02.322
your source.

00:02:02.572 --> 00:02:04.720
In the workbook, record what you actually

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:06.945
observed and which proposal version you

00:02:06.945 --> 00:02:07.436
used.

00:02:07.686 --> 00:02:10.159
If something differs, keep it in Draft and

00:02:10.159 --> 00:02:11.910
resolve the mismatch with Sales.

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:14.833
After saving a correction, reopen the draft

00:02:14.833 --> 00:02:16.448
and repeat the affected checks.

00:02:17.467 --> 00:02:19.736
The handoff now has three parts: the

00:02:19.736 --> 00:02:22.136
proposal version, the matching draft, and

00:02:22.136 --> 00:02:23.248
your recorded checks.

00:02:23.498 --> 00:02:26.046
In this example, Sales Ops owns the next

00:02:26.046 --> 00:02:28.938
action: request the Sales manager's review.

00:02:29.188 --> 00:02:31.108
We haven't submitted that request here.

00:02:31.358 --> 00:02:34.131
If the proposal changes, update the source

00:02:34.131 --> 00:02:35.817
and recheck the affected terms.

00:02:36.833 --> 00:02:39.856
If you use PandaDoc or Docusign, keep the

00:02:39.856 --> 00:02:42.056
signed document as evidence of customer

00:02:42.056 --> 00:02:42.657
agreement.

00:02:42.907 --> 00:02:45.376
For an external agreement, getting its terms

00:02:45.376 --> 00:02:47.926
into the native Contract needs a create or

00:02:47.926 --> 00:02:50.451
import step, or a separately tested

00:02:50.451 --> 00:02:51.206
integration.

00:02:51.456 --> 00:02:53.904
A document-completion workflow can start a

00:02:53.904 --> 00:02:55.573
review task when configured;

00:02:55.823 --> 00:02:58.321
renewal follow-up needs its own dates, owner

00:02:58.321 --> 00:02:59.045
and testing.

00:02:59.295 --> 00:03:01.964
The PDF outlines those proposed follow-up

00:03:01.964 --> 00:03:02.473
checks.

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:05.944
For your next draft, use the workbook beside

00:03:05.944 --> 00:03:08.094
this video to record your checks and next

00:03:08.094 --> 00:03:08.513
owner.

00:03:08.763 --> 00:03:10.911
Use the PDF when you're ready to plan

00:03:10.911 --> 00:03:12.838
the signing and renewal follow-up.
